- Story & Context: Named after the legendary projectionist who lost his thumb to a high-speed film rewinder, this track samples MacIntyre's own raspy voice recorded through a telephone line from his hospital bed. MacIntyre refused pain medication, insisting that the steady, high-pitched ringing in his ears was the exact frequency needed to resolve a stubborn D-minor bagpipe chord. "Patch my pipe into the beam and let it bleed!" he yelled over the receiver while Fiona cut the tape splice directly into a 172 BPM slicing engine, creating the scene’s most iconic, tear-jerking anthem.
